---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Section from '../../components/layout/Section.astro';
import Container from '../../components/layout/Container.astro';
import WaitlistForm from '../../components/forms/WaitlistForm.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Airports"
  description="Runway is built around the rhythm of airport openings: phased turnover, multiple consultants, regulatory gates, BHS commissioning, and live-ops trial cycles."
>
  <Section tone="cream">
    <Container width="narrow">
      <header class="head stack-l">
        <p class="eyebrow">Solutions · Airports</p>
        <h1 class="display step-5">Terminals, BHS, and transit handovers that have to land on a fixed date.</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Airport openings are the canonical ORAT problem: dozens of consultants, hundreds of
          trade interfaces, regulatory acceptance gates, and a fixed open-for-traffic date that
          can't slip. Runway is built around that rhythm.
        </p>
      </header>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section tone="paper">
    <Container width="narrow">
      <div class="block stack-l">
        <h2 class="display step-3">What gets shipped pre-configured</h2>
        <ul class="bullets">
          <li><strong>Functional area libraries:</strong> passenger flow, BHS, concessions, wayfinding, IT &amp; comms, security checkpoints, gate operations, ground service equipment</li>
          <li><strong>Stakeholder types:</strong> airline ops representatives, TSA coordinators, FBO managers, baggage commissioning agents, ATC liaisons</li>
          <li><strong>Trial templates:</strong> baggage flow trials, passenger flow trials, fire-life-safety trials, irregular ops trials</li>
          <li><strong>ORAT phase template:</strong> the 8-phase model pre-seeded with airport-typical durations and gate criteria</li>
          <li><strong>Construction phase scoping:</strong> Concourse Construction, BHS Expansion, Transit Expansion — each scopable to its own consultant team</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section tone="cream">
    <Container width="narrow">
      <div class="block stack-l">
        <h2 class="display step-3">The cascade that matters</h2>
        <p>
          The classic airport ORAT failure: BHS commissioning needs IT cutover. IT cutover needs
          security wiring. Security wiring needs final ceiling close-out. Final close-out needs
          the punch list. One slip on the punch list, and the entire trial schedule shifts —
          but nobody knows by how much until someone manually walks the dependency tree.
        </p>
        <p>
          Runway's offset engine walks it automatically. The trial that depended on BHS shows a
          new date and a chevron pointing at the punch list as the driver. The team is having a
          recovery conversation in minutes, not days.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section tone="paper">
    <Container width="narrow">
      <div class="block stack-l">
        <h2 class="display step-3">Why airport teams pick Runway</h2>
        <ul class="reasons">
          <li>
            <strong>One workspace across the program.</strong> Concourse, BHS, and transit teams
            share data without sharing keystrokes — each consultant scoped to their construction
            phase, the program owner sees the rollup.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Trial execution that's actually usable on the floor.</strong> Objective-by-objective
            pass / fail / blocked logging, attendee check-in, Excel export for the operations
            handover packet.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Zone readiness on the floor plan.</strong> Click a concourse, see its
            readiness percentage rolled up from linked activities. Hover for the blockers.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>External ID for the cast of consultants.</strong> No procurement cycle for
            new logins. Invite a contractor by email; Entra External ID handles the rest.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section tone="ink">
    <Container width="narrow">
      <div class="cta stack-l">
        <p class="eyebrow">Running an airport opening?</p>
        <h2 class="display step-3">We'd like to talk to you specifically.</h2>
        <WaitlistForm source="airports" showMessage cta="Get in touch" />
      </div>
    </Container>
  </Section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .step-5 { font-size: var(--step-5); }
  .step-3 { font-size: var(--step-3); }
  .head { max-width: 50rem; }
  .block { max-width: 50rem; }
  .block p { color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: var(--step-0); line-height: 1.55; }
  .bullets { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-s); padding-left: var(--space-m); list-style: disc; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .reasons { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-m); list-style: none; }
  .reasons li { padding-left: var(--space-m); border-left: 2px solid var(--accent); color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .cta { text-align: center; align-items: center; }
  .cta .eyebrow { color: var(--accent-soft); }
  .cta :global(.waitlist) { max-width: 28rem; margin-inline: auto; width: 100%; }
</style>
